  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a conceptual framework linking several soil concepts—soil fertility, soil quality, soil health, and soil security—showing them as nested, increasingly broad ideas. The arc-shaped layers suggest that soil fertility is the narrowest concept, while soil security is the most comprehensive, integrating wider environmental and societal concerns. A bar along the bottom indicates that broader concepts have greater relevance to the Sustainable Development Goals.\n\nThe right side organizes these ideas by scale, from pedon and field to local, regional, national, and global levels. As scale broadens, the associated functions and services also expand: from primary productivity at field level, through nutrient and water cycling, to carbon sequestration and habitat provisioning at larger scales. The listed services similarly move from plant production and economic viability to water quality, biodiversity, climate control, human health, policy, and culture.\n\nThe figure also shows a shift in who is most affected: farmers and land users at small scales, neighbours and the public at intermediate scales, and policymakers at national and global scales. At the bottom, labels imply a parallel shift from private property toward common good, and from quantitative assessment toward more multidimensional, qualitative evaluation.",
